<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Potala Palace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1645">1645</a>–<a href="/years/1694">1694</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Potala Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, was the winter residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lamas</a> and the seat of the Tibetan government. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">Fifth Dalai Lama</a> began it in 1645 on the Red Hill, on the site of an earlier palace attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a>, and the central Red Palace was completed in 1694. With over a thousand rooms, tombs of Dalai Lamas and many chapels, it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/potala-palace.jpg" alt="Potala Palace" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lhasa Government · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Potala_Palace_HQ.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The palace is named after Mount Potalaka, regarded in Buddhist tradition as the abode of the bodhisattva <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avalokitesvara">Avalokiteśvara</a>. It stands on Marpo Ri, the Red Hill, in the centre of the Lhasa Valley, on the site of an earlier palace attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a> and traditionally dated to 637. Two chapels at its northwest corner, the Phakpa Lhakhang and the Chogyel Drupuk, preserve parts of that palace; the second is a cavern identified as Songtsen Gampo's meditation cave.</p><p>Ngawang Lozang Gyatso, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">Fifth Dalai Lama</a>, began the present building in 1645 after his adviser Konchog Chophel, Thirty-fifth Ganden Tripa of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school, pointed out that the site lay between Drepung and Sera monasteries and the old city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> and suited a seat of government. The outer structure took three years, the interior and its furnishings 45. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a> and his government moved into the White Palace in 1649, and the Red Palace was added between 1690 and 1694.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Potala is built in the dzong style at about 3,700 metres and measures 400 metres from east to west and 350 from north to south. Its sloping stone walls average 3 metres thick and 5 at the base, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> was poured into the foundations as protection against earthquakes. The complex rises thirteen stories and holds more than 1,000 rooms, 10,000 shrines and some 200,000 statues. The crimson Red Palace contains the principal halls, chapels and shrines of past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lamas</a>, and a Kalachakra Mandala was made there in the 1690s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>From 1649 until 1959 the palace was the residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lamas</a>. It was damaged in the Tibetan uprising of 1959, when Chinese shells were fired into its windows, and it became a museum complex after the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>. It escaped damage in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> in 1966 through the intervention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a>. The Potala Palace was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 1994, after a restoration in 1989–1994.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Potala Palace” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…January 1940 the Regent Reting Rinpoche asked the Chinese central government to exempt him from the lot-drawing with the Golden Urn, which it approved, and after a ransom of 400,000 silver dragons was paid to Ma Bufang he travelled to Lhasa and was enthroned at the <b>Potala Palace</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet (1912-1951) · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The government took its name from the Dalai Lama's residence at Drepung monastery and soon moved to the <b>Potala Palace</b>, built in Lhasa from 1645.</blockquote><a href="/state/ganden-phodrang">Ganden Phodrang</a></li></ul></section>
